WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
On the Home screen, click Open project… and choose the project of the first tutorial.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
The project opens up to date: one night, 436 lights, 1.8 h of light.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Click Post.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Post shows the picture of the first tutorial. Before any gesture, look at what more light could give.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.000
At 100 %, in the faint gas, a grain: the light of one night, 1.8 h, is what the picture is made of.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Click Graph.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Click the card of the workflow’s first block, so that the palette works on that workflow.

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
In the palette, double-click Session.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:42.000
The window says: Session 2 added to “Color minimal”: it stacks with its sessions, and Calibration added: its stack is calibrated together. It is a second session of the same workflow.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Click Table.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.000
The Table lists the sessions of the stack: the new one is empty, and named Session 2 like the first, Session 1.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Click the new session’s name and press F2, then type its night.

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
The second session is named 28 Dec 2024, the night of its frames.

14
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Name the first session the same way: 29 Dec 2024.

15
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Click Inspect, with the new session selected.

16
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
In the Lights group, click +.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Click Add folder… and choose the folder of the night of 28 December: 358 lights.

18
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
The 358 lights of the second night are added: 15 s, ISO 4000, 1.5 h of light.

19
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.000
In the Darks group, click +.

20
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Choose Use the frames of “29 Dec 2024”.

21
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Do the same in the Flats group.

22
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:34.000
The darks and the flats are those of the first night, shared with it: one master each. The two nights come from the same camera, with the same settings and the same telescope, a day apart.

23
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Click Run. The button gives the estimate of the computation.

24
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The Run project window lists what is computed: the calibration of the frames, the registration, the stacking and the blocks that follow.

25
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Not shown in real time: the computation took 38 min on an RTX 4060 Ti graphics card (16 GB).

26
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Calibration 12.6 min for the 794 frames of the two nights, registration 2.4 min, stacking 22.3 min, the rest under 1 min.

27
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
The two-night project is opened: on the Home screen, Open project….

28
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Selection reads 754 of 794 frames kept. The two nights are turned by 178° and register with a residual of 0.29 px.

29
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Click Report.

30
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The report reads 754 frames of 15 s, 3 h 08 of light, against 1.8 h with one night.

31
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Click Post.

32
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Post is unchanged: the same layer, the same sliders. At the fitted size the picture is the same.

33
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:39.000
At 100 %, the same faint gas has less grain: the noise is 3 to 12 % lower.

34
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Click Export…

35
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Choose JPEG in Format.

36
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Click Export.

37
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.000
The image is written, with its processing history beside it.
